3. GitHub setup: step by step

- **Open the mini-project repository: **Sign in at https://github.com, open the Team 6 IPP repository, and confirm it is the correct repository.

- **Create a Project: **Use the repository Projects tab and choose New project, or create a project from your profile/organization under Projects. Name it “IPP – Internship & Placement Preparation Portal”. A Board or Table layout is suitable.

- **Link the repository: **If the Project was created from your profile/organization, open Project settings and add/link the IPP repository. If created from the repository Projects tab, verify the repository is connected.

- **Enable Issues: **Open repository Settings → General → Features and enable Issues if it is not visible and you have permission.

- **Create backlog issues: **For every requirement in Section 4 of this document, create one GitHub Issue. Use the requirement ID in the title so the issue can be traced back to the SRS.

- **Add issues to the Project: **In the Project, choose Add item and add each existing repository issue. Verify that all 36 issues appear.

- **Create useful labels: **Suggested labels: functional-requirement, non-functional-requirement, security, priority-high, priority-medium, sprint-1, sprint-2. Reuse labels rather than creating duplicates.

- **Add Story Points field: **In the Project table, use + / Add field → Number, name it “Story Points”, and enter the estimate listed for each issue below. If your instructor has specified a different scale, use that scale instead.

- **Assign owners: **Open each issue and select the listed GitHub username under Assignees. Make sure each teammate has repository access; names in this document must be matched to their actual GitHub accounts.

- **Set up sprint planning: **In Project settings, create Iterations named Sprint 1 and Sprint 2 with the official dates supplied by the instructor. If Iterations are unavailable, create a single-select Sprint field with Backlog, Sprint 1, and Sprint 2.

- **Set status columns: **Use a Status field with Backlog, Todo, In Progress, and Done. During the dry run, practise moving issues through the workflow; only mark genuinely completed work as Done.

4. Backlog: requirements, story points, owners and proposed sprint

The SRS specifies 24 functional requirements (IPP-F-001 to IPP-F-024), six non-functional requirements (IPP-NF-001 to IPP-NF-006), and six security requirements (IPP-SR-001 to IPP-SR-006). This plan creates one issue per requirement: 36 issues total. Story points below are proposed relative estimates for team discussion, not values stated in the SRS. Sprint placement is provisional until official sprint dates and team capacity are confirmed.

